n":0.10078,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":67.2,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Allegiant Travel Company, a leisure travel company, provides travel and leisure services and products to residents of under-served cities in the United States. The company offers scheduled air transportation on limited-frequency, nonstop flights between underserved cities and leisure destinations. As of February 1, 2026, it operated a fleet of 106 Airbus A320 series aircraft and 16 Boeing 737 series aircraft. The company also provides air-related services and products in conjunction with air transportation, including larger seats, baggage fees, advance seat assignments, travel protection products, change fees, priority boarding, customer convenience fee, food and beverage purchases on board, and other air-related services, as well as use of its call center for purchases. In addition, it offers third party travel products, such as hotel rooms, rental cars, and travel insurance from a third-party insurer; air transportation services; and charter services on a year-round and ad-hoc basis. Further, the company owns and operates a golf course. Allegiant Travel Company was founded in 1997 and is based in Las Vegas, Nevada.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Gregory Clark Anderson CPA | CEO & Director | Age 43 | Compensation: $2.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $317K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $9K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $1K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 6\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Allegiant Travel Company is a mid-cap industrials business with a $2.5B market cap on $2.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +8% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 1% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $75M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 3 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin 0.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.5%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 10% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.1\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (10.9% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.0},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 38% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Valued at a 23% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Allegiant Travel (ALGT) vs. Its Peers Critical Analysis","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/12/allegiant-travel-algt-vs-its-peers-critical-analysis.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794532515.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.7,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Here's Why Allegiant Travel (ALGT) is a Strong Growth Stock","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3028655/here-s-why-allegiant-travel-algt-is-a-strong-growth-stock?cid=CS-ZC-FT-fundamental_analysis%7Czacks_education_growth_score-3028655","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794491102.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.71,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Donald Smith & Co. Reports 6.3% Ownership Stake in Allegiant Travel Co.","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/donald-smith-co-reports-63-ownership-stake-in-allegiant-travel-co","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794586072.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":1,"scope":"company","tags":["m_and_a"]},{"title":"The Bet Allegiant Travel Holders Made","